# ArduRocket SITL defaults: a fin-steered solid-motor rocket.
#
# The airframe has no RC receiver and no ground station in flight. It holds
# vertical on four fins and nothing can command it to do anything else.

# --- Frame ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which actuator steers the airframe. 0 = Fins (the only implemented option).
# 1 = ThrustVectoring is scoped but NOT built; selecting it makes the vehicle
# refuse to boot rather than fly with the wrong mixer. Reboot required to change.
FRAME_CLASS      0

# --- Fins -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Four fins on the first four outputs, numbered clockwise viewed from the nose.
SERVO1_FUNCTION  190
SERVO2_FUNCTION  191
SERVO3_FUNCTION  192
SERVO4_FUNCTION  193

# --- Flight stage detection -------------------------------------------------
RKT_ENABLE       1
# Launch: the pad reads 1g, the motor pulls several. 1.5g with 50ms of debounce
# clears pad vibration without waiting long enough to matter.
RKT_LAUNCH_G     1.5
RKT_LAUNCH_MS    50
# Body X points at the nose once AHRS_ORIENTATION is applied.
RKT_LAUNCH_AX    0
# Burnout: coasting reads near zero (drag only), boosting reads several g.
# Recorded only -- it does not stop the fins. Apogee does.
RKT_BURN_G       0.2
RKT_BURN_MS      100
# Apogee: once launched, a sustained negative climb rate means we are over the top
# and the fins must stop. This -- not burnout -- ends steering.
RKT_APOG_MS      500

# --- Fin gain scheduling ----------------------------------------------------
# The mixer scales fin deflection by min(MOT_Q_REF/q, MOT_GAIN_MAX). Because the
# fin output still clips at +/-1, MOT_Q_REF effectively sets how much of the fins'
# physically available authority the controller is allowed to use at speed.
#
# 2500 Pa. This is the single most important control number, and it was raised from an
# earlier 600 once the attitude estimate was made trustworthy (see the estimator section):
# at 600 the schedule crushed the fin command to ~5% during high-q boost (600/~5000 Pa),
# so the airframe's 20-deg launch lean was never corrected in the window where the fins are
# strongest, and it gravity-turned away to 100 deg by apogee. At 2500 the fins keep ~half
# their command at boost q, and the airframe HOLDS its launch attitude (~20 deg off a 20-deg
# rail) through the flight instead of tumbling. Low-q behaviour is unchanged -- MOT_GAIN_MAX
# still caps the boost near rail exit.
#
# The reason it must be high: the weathercock moment the fins fight grows with q, so a
# schedule that holds the CONTROL moment constant (small deflection at high q) loses at high
# q. Raising MOT_Q_REF lets the fins keep authority where the disturbance is largest. Off a
# NEAR-VERTICAL rail the required correction is small and it holds tight; the 20-deg case is
# the worst legal one. History: 142 (rail-exit q, good for loop stability, starved
# everything above rail exit) -> 600 -> 2500 -> 5000 -> 12000.
#
# 12000: paired with the tilt-law INTEGRAL term (RKT_TILT_I) and the spin damper gain
# (RKT_SPIN_DAMP, compile-time in rocket_control.cpp). At 5000 the direct tilt law only drove
# the steady TRUE tilt to ~6 deg off a 20-deg rail; at 12000 the fins keep near-full authority
# through boost q and the integral nulls the residual weathercock lean -> steady TRUE tilt ~1.3
# deg (mission PASS). RKT_SPIN_DAMP was set to 0.042 = 0.10*5000/12000 to keep the spin MOMENT
# loop-gain constant as this rose, so the two MUST move together -- if you change MOT_Q_REF,
# rescale RKT_SPIN_DAMP by the inverse ratio or the spin will chatter (5 Hz limit cycle) or run.
MOT_Q_REF        12000

# Upper bound on the low-q gain boost. This cannot create authority: the output
# clips at +/-1 regardless, so the hard ceiling is Kf/|Ka| of angle of attack --
# about 11 deg for this airframe at 2 calibers of static margin.
MOT_GAIN_MAX     4

# --- Attitude control -------------------------------------------------------
# SITL-tuned tilt gains (not yet flown). With the spin killed and the estimate clean (see the
# spin damper in rocket_control.cpp), these actively steer the 20-deg launch lean DOWN toward
# vertical through the powered flight, instead of merely holding it. The cascade is
# fin ~= ATC_ANG_P * angle_err * ATC_RAT_P.
#   RAT_I 0.3  -- a FLIGHT integrator: it winds up the steady fin needed to null the lean
#     against the airframe's weathercock. Safe because the flight code resets the rate I-terms
#     while on the rail (rkt.hold_integrators()), so it is zero on the pad and only active once
#     flying -- the earlier "no integrator" note was over-cautious.
# View roll and view pitch are the two tilt axes. View yaw is spin about the long axis; it is
# now ACTIVELY damped by a direct gyro spin damper in rocket_control.cpp (RKT_SPIN_DAMP), NOT
# by these ATC_*_YAW gains -- leaving the spin uncontrolled let it run away to 1200 deg/s and
# coning-corrupt the attitude estimate, which was the root cause of the vehicle not flying
# vertical. Getting the last few degrees to dead-vertical is further tilt-gain refinement.
ATC_RAT_RLL_P    0.8
ATC_RAT_RLL_I    0.6
ATC_RAT_RLL_D    0.04
ATC_RAT_PIT_P    0.8
ATC_RAT_PIT_I    0.6
ATC_RAT_PIT_D    0.04
ATC_RAT_YAW_P    0.10
ATC_RAT_YAW_I    0.01
ATC_RAT_YAW_D    0.0
ATC_ANG_RLL_P    18.0
ATC_ANG_PIT_P    18.0
ATC_ANG_YAW_P    2.0

# --- Loop rate --------------------------------------------------------------
# A rocket needs the fast 400Hz control loop, and it must match the SITL IMU rate.
SCHED_LOOP_RATE  400

# --- Arming -----------------------------------------------------------------
# ARMING_SKIPCHK is a bitmask of pre-arm checks to SKIP.
# NOTE: the parameter is ARMING_SKIPCHK, NOT the old ARMING_CHECK. Setting the
# wrong name fails silently -- the value is simply ignored and every check runs.
#
# BRING-UP SETTING (current): -1 skips every check, so the vehicle always arms.
# This is required in SITL, where the simulated board has no accelerometer
# calibration and so could never satisfy the INS check. Do NOT fly this value.
ARMING_SKIPCHK   -1

# REAL FLIGHT: skip only the checks for hardware this airframe does not have, and
# keep the ones that would actually save it. Swap the line above for this one:
#
#   ARMING_SKIPCHK 21064
#
# 21064 = GPS(8) + RC(64) + AIRSPEED(512) + GPS_CONFIG(4096) + MISSION(16384)
#
#   skipped, because the hardware is absent:
#     GPS(8), GPS_CONFIG(4096)  - no GPS, so the EKF never gets a home
#     RC(64)                    - no receiver
#     AIRSPEED(512)             - no pitot
#     MISSION(16384)            - no mission
#
#   KEPT, because they are the safety net that matters:
#     BARO(2)   - apogee detection rides on the barometer. A dead baro means the
#                 fins never stop and thrash all the way down.
#     INS(16)   - attitude hold depends entirely on the IMU.
#     plus COMPASS(4), PARAMETERS(32), VOLTAGE(128), BATTERY(256),
#          LOGGING(1024), SYSTEM(8192)
#
# Consequence: keeping INS(16) means the "3D Accel calibration needed" check stays
# active, so the flight controller must have a real 3D accelerometer calibration
# before it will arm. That is the check doing its job, not an obstacle.
#
# Regardless of this parameter, AP_Arming_Rocket separately enforces that the
# airframe is near-vertical and still (i.e. actually on the rail) before arming.

# --- Levelling to vertical --------------------------------------------------
# The target is ALWAYS true vertical. The rail attitude captured at arming is a
# calibration: the attitude target blends from it to vertical as dynamic pressure
# rises, so the correction happens as the fins gain authority rather than as a step
# at rail exit where they have almost none.
#
# 600 Pa is about 31 m/s -- shortly after the 50 fps (15.24 m/s, 142 Pa) rail exit,
# so the rocket is commanding true vertical early in the boost.
# Lower = level out sooner and harder. 0 = command vertical immediately, no blend.
RKT_LEVEL_Q      0

# Stop driving the fins below this dynamic pressure. Below it they cannot produce a
# useful moment however far they deflect, so steering there only wastes travel and
# winds up the integrators. 50 Pa is about 9 m/s. Set 0 to steer all the way to apogee.
#
# NOTE this does NOT eliminate large fin deflections during coast: measured coast
# travel is dominated by genuine correction at q well ABOVE this threshold, where the
# fins do have authority. This guards the true zero-authority tail only.
RKT_MIN_Q        50

# --- Airframe ---------------------------------------------------------------
# How the FLIGHT CONTROLLER BOARD is rotated relative to the airframe -- NOT how the
# airframe sits on the pad. 0 = board X axis points out the nose, the conventional
# mounting, same as a plane. Change this only if the board is physically rotated in
# the airframe.
#
# It is NOT the job of this parameter to express "the rocket stands vertical". That is
# done by the AP_AHRS_View(ROTATION_PITCH_90) the controller flies on, which presents
# the nose-up airframe as level. Setting BOTH rotates twice: this was 24 (Pitch90),
# which stacked on the view's 90 degrees and read a vertical rail as 88.8 degrees off
# vertical. It went unnoticed only because AHRS_* was never registered, so the value
# was silently discarded -- a wrong setting hidden by a dead one.
AHRS_ORIENTATION 0

# --- Compass: DISPLAY ONLY, never in the flight solution --------------------
# The compass hardware stays enabled so the driver keeps reading it, but the EKF is
# told not to use it for yaw. The flight code needs no heading at all: attitude hold
# works on tilt, and spin is held by commanding yaw RATE zero off the gyro.
#
# The magnetometer is instead read directly in GCS_MAVLink_Rocket.cpp to produce a
# heading for the ground station. That keeps a real compass rose on the pad without
# ever letting a magnetically hostile environment -- steel motor casing, igniter
# current -- reach the attitude solution the fins fly on.
COMPASS_USE      0
COMPASS_USE2     0
COMPASS_USE3     0

# No receiver. Without this the vehicle waits for RC it will never see.
RC_PROTOCOLS     0

# --- Attitude estimator: DCM, not EKF3 --------------------------------------
# This airframe has NO GPS (see below), which is the hard case for attitude estimation:
# with no velocity reference an estimator cannot separate the vehicle's own acceleration
# from gravity. Under boost the motor pulls tens of g along the nose, swamping the 1 g of
# gravity the estimator uses to find "down".
#
# EKF3 cannot handle this without GPS. In SITL its tilt estimate glitched by up to 90 deg
# and then held a large standing bias -- it reported a phantom 117 deg tilt while the true
# tilt was 30. Retuning its process noise (EK3_ACC_P_NSE / GYRO_P_NSE) removed the glitches
# but left a ~12 deg error it could not shed. The failure is structural -- no velocity
# aiding to observe tilt -- not a tuning problem.
#
# DCM holds attitude here, the way the flown MatrixPilot fin-steered rocket did: pure
# gyro + accelerometer, no GPS. AHRS_EKF_TYPE 0 selects it. Vertical velocity -- needed for
# apogee detection and the fin gain schedule -- then comes from the barometer's filtered
# climb rate automatically (DCM's get_vert_pos_rate_D() falls back to AP_Baro when there is
# no GPS velocity), so no code change is needed to feed it. EKF3 still runs as a dormant
# fallback.
AHRS_EKF_TYPE    0
#
# The KEY that makes DCM work under thrust and steering is a code gate, not a parameter:
# ArduRocket calls ahrs.set_attitude_gyro_only(true) from BOOST through DESCENT, and the DCM
# backend then STOPS using the accelerometer for attitude and coasts on the gyro. This is
# exactly the flown MatrixPilot rocket's technique (rmat.c: accel correction only while
# `launched == 0`): once the motor lights, the accelerometer measures thrust and fin-steering
# force, not gravity, so it is a false "down" and must be ignored. On the pad the gate is off
# and DCM aligns to true vertical from gravity. See rocket_control.cpp and AP_AHRS_DCM.cpp.
#
# AHRS_RP_P (how fast the accelerometer corrects attitude) therefore only matters ON THE PAD
# now -- in flight the accelerometer is gated out entirely -- so it is left at the default.
AHRS_RP_P        0.2

# --- GPS: NONE --------------------------------------------------------------
# There is no GPS on the flight controller, and it stays off (GPS1_TYPE 0). Two reasons:
# NOTE: the parameter is GPS1_TYPE, not GPS_TYPE. The old GPS_TYPE name no longer exists, so a
# "GPS_TYPE 0" line is silently ignored and the GPS stays ON at its default -- which put EKF3
# on GPS and hid the whole no-GPS design for an entire debugging session. Use GPS1_TYPE.
#   1. A GPS receiver loses lock under high-g boost -- exactly when control matters most --
#      and a dropout mid-boost is worse than never trusting it.
#   2. With DCM as the estimator (above), a PRESENT GPS actively corrupts the attitude: its
#      velocity feeds DCM's centripetal-acceleration correction, which pollutes the tilt
#      once the fins start steering. In SITL, GPS-present made the estimate diverge past
#      100 deg of error; GPS fully off held it within a few degrees. (AHRS_GPS_USE=0 only
#      gates GPS navigation, not that correction, so it is not enough -- the receiver must
#      be off.)
# Finding the airframe after landing is an OFF-BOARD job (a separate recovery tracker), not
# the flight controller's.
#
# Consequence: no position solution, so no home is ever set. That is expected.
# GLOBAL_POSITION_INT will not be valid. Arming resets the height datum to the rail.
GPS1_TYPE        0
#
# Keep EKF3 (the dormant fallback) from ever using GPS for horizontal state -- baro for
# height only -- should it ever become primary.
EK3_SRC1_POSXY   0
EK3_SRC1_VELXY   0
EK3_SRC1_POSZ    1

# Outputs live at boot: there is no safety switch on the pad.
BRD_SAFETY_DEFLT 0
